  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nAmple cloud cover this morning from a very robust and shallower \nmarine layer, with satellite still showing some inland coverage as \nof 11am. Areas of patchy fog were reported, mostly in part thanks to \nincreasing heights as a weak ridge attempts to take hold of SoCal \nand a shortwave trough moves eastward today. The morning sounding \nalready shows drier air in the mid and upper levels with the \ndeparture of the trough, also evident on mid-level water vapor \nsatellite with additional subsidence 150 miles southwest of San \nDiego. This lack of moisture will be a main limiting factor for \nstorms in the mountains this afternoon, although a few thunderstorms \nremain possible for primarily the San Diego and Riverside County \nMountains. Anything that develops will be diurnally driven and \nunlikely to produce flash flooding, with storms ending by sunset. \n\nSouthern California will be in a push-and-pull for the remainder of \nthe week between a deepening low over the PacNW, high pressure over \nTexas, and two tropical cyclones in the eastern Pacific. The \naforementioned weak ridge will hold through at least Friday and keep \ntemperatures well above normal with highs in the 90s for inland and \nHigh Desert, 80s for the coast and mountains, and 100-103 for the \nlow deserts. Things start to change this weekend as the ridge is \ncompressed and the high over Texas shifts southward, allowing for \ntropical moisture to shift up Baja and into Southern California and \nwestern Arizona. This could mean some very warm morning temperatures \nboth Saturday and Sunday as the moisture reduces radiational cooling, \nand temperatures in the afternoon remain elevated as heights will be \nslow to fall. This is likely to bring about some Moderate HeatRisk \nfor the coastal areas on Saturday with temperatures around 10-12 \ndegrees above normal and warm overnight lows above 71 degrees. A lot \nof this will depend heavily on cloud cover associated with the \ntropical cyclones well to our south, which could impact peak heating \non Saturday, limiting our warming.\n\nBy late weekend, a trough off the aforementioned larger low in the \nPacNW will swing down and become a closed low by Monday. This could \nhelp initiate some convection while mixing with the influx of \nmoisture. For now, much of any precipitation looks to stay east of \nthe region, as drier air on the backside of the trough looks to push \nin as tropical moisture is shunted east of the mountains. Should \nSoCal get dry slotted (80-90% confidence), Sunday and Monday would at \nleast feature cooler weather thanks to the deepening low as it \nmeanders into Arizona and Utah. Meanwhile, other states in the \nDesert SW may experience multiple rounds of precipitation and some \nheavy rainfall. At this point, the tracks of Polo and Odalys and \nwhere the larger upper level pattern will be are not conducive to \nprecipitation for SoCal. \n\nWith building high pressure to the west and drier air filter in, the \nregion will return to a warming trend after Monday along with \nquieter weather.\n\nA few impacts that may be observed early next week due to the \ntropical cyclones: \n-higher humidity for the deserts, especially the low deserts, late \nweekend into early next week. \n-coastal flooding and coastal erosion, especially for south-facing \nbeaches, Sunday through Tuesday. Additional details on this can be \nfound in the Beaches discussion below.",
